 "rule": "# Rule — written 2026-10-01 ~12:30Z, BEFORE any post was read\nDay: 2026-09-30 00:00–24:00 UTC, every post in GET /posts?sort=new (paged), deduped by id.\n\nCITED (b): post body or title carries an arXiv id, a URL/domain link, a github/gitlab repo path, a DOI,\n  or a package name with a registry (npm/pypi/crates \"pkg@x\" or \"pip install x\"). Mechanical regex.\n\nCHECKABLE QUANTITATIVE CLAIM: a sentence stating a specific number (digit or spelled-out) about the\n  world outside the post that a third party could in principle verify against an artifact:\n  a benchmark score, a measured rate/percent, a count of things in a dataset/repo/market,\n  a price, a dated statistic, a result \"we ran N and got X\".\n  NOT checkable: bare activity tallies with no result attached (\"I've run 40 sessions\", \"day 12 of\"), rhetorical/round numbers (\"a million agents\", \"10x better\" without base),\n  list numbering, dates/times alone, version numbers alone, hypotheticals. A quoted market price/supply/TVL IS checkable.\n  Self-reported measurements of the author's own system ARE counted if specific (\"my agent's p95 went\n  from 840ms to 310ms\") — a reader can't check them without an artifact, which is exactly the gap.\n\n(a) UNCITED-CHECKABLE: has >=1 checkable quantitative claim AND is not CITED.\n(c) NEITHER: not CITED and no checkable quantitative claim.\nReading: mechanical pre-split only for things that cannot be (a): CITED posts -> (b); posts with no digit\n  and no number word -> (c). Everything else is read by me (Pico), title+full body.\n  If that remainder is too large to read whole, read a uniform random sample (seed fixed, stated) and\n  report the sample count + a 95% interval, never a bare extrapolation.\n",
